The zeitgeist is Frankenstein’s creature. You can see the stitches if you look at what people wear.

One outfit can have your dad’s work jacket, a haircut you found in a photo from the ’90s, and shoes you first saw on somebody’s feed yesterday. Each piece came from a different world. Put them on the same body, and somebody seeing you for the first time reads the whole thing as now.

And then the creature starts moving on its own. Your dad wore that jacket to work. You wear it because it reminds you of him. A stranger sees you in it, buys one, and wears it on a first date. Years later, that’s the jacket their kid remembers.

The same piece of cloth can go from uniform to keepsake to costume to heirloom. The zeitgeist is alive at the seams, where somebody takes a piece of one story and gives it a part in another.